  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)={2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2).$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=-5.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =-5+{\\int }_{1}^{3}({2}^{2t-1}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5+{[{2}^{2t-2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{2(3)-2}-{2}^{2(1)-2} \\\\  & =-5+{2}^{4}-{2}^{0} \\\\  & =-5+16-1 \\\\  & =-5+15 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=10.$",
